  "userdoc": {
    "kind": "user",
    "methods": {
      "COOLDOWN()": {
        "notice": "Release cooldown. Blocks a second Safe from taking a name the         moment it is freed, not the previous owner from returning."
      },
      "assignReserved(string,address,address)": {
        "notice": "Assign a reserved `handle` to `safe`, at `recordsLocation`."
      },
      "authorGate()": {
        "notice": "The gate asked about `msg.sender` at registration time."
      },
      "check(string)": {
        "notice": "Whether `handle` can be registered right now, and why not."
      },
      "entry(string)": {
        "notice": "The live entry for `handle`, or a zeroed entry when it is free."
      },
      "governance()": {
        "notice": "The only address allowed to reserve, assign and move the gate."
      },
      "nameOf(address)": {
        "notice": "The handle `who` holds, or the empty string."
      },
      "register(string,address)": {
        "notice": "Claim `handle` for `msg.sender`, pointing at `recordsLocation`."
      },
      "release()": {
        "notice": "Give up `msg.sender`'s handle. The entry is deleted, the skeleton         is freed, and the cooldown starts. The version stays in         `lastVersion`, so the next registration of this handle continues         the sequence instead of reusing one."
      },
      "reserve(string[])": {
        "notice": "Reserve the skeletons of `handles` so public registration refuses         them. Each handle must pass the same structure check `register`         applies; a spelling no author could register is refused rather         than reserved as an unmatchable skeleton."
      },
      "resolve(string)": {
        "notice": "The Safe holding `handle`, or zero. A released name resolves to         zero even though its version is retained."
      },
      "setAuthorGate(address)": {
        "notice": "Point the registry at a new author gate. Non-zero and with code:         this is the contract every registration trusts."
      },
      "setRecordsLocation(address)": {
        "notice": "Move the owning Safe's records pointer, e.g. after the Safe         installs a new records module."
      },
      "skeletonOf(string)": {
        "notice": "The skeleton of `handle`: lowercased, `-` removed, then `0→o`,         `1→l`, `i→l`, `5→s`, `rn→m`, `vv→w`, one pass over the         hyphen-free bytes."
      },
      "unreserve(string[])": {
        "notice": "Return `handles`' skeletons to public registration. Validated for         the same reason `reserve` is: an invalid spelling names no         skeleton an author could hold."
      },
      "versionOf(string)": {
        "notice": "The last version ever assigned to `handle`, 0 if never         registered. Survives release on purpose: versions are never         reused. For the live entry's version use `entry(handle).version`."
      }
    },
    "notice": "Author handles on Base. A handle is the unique name; the pen name the         CLI writes is free text and `.s2s` is display only. Nothing here is         ENS or DNS: the mapping is this contract's storage.         The registered owner of a handle is a Safe (`msg.sender`), and a Safe         is accepted only when `S2SAuthorGate` says it is an author holding the         records module named at registration. The gate's allowlists are         constructor arguments of the gate, not of this contract.         Handle rules are byte rules on the raw UTF-8 bytes, checked in this         order: length 3..32 bytes, then `a-z 0-9 -` only (uppercase is         rejected, never folded), then no leading, trailing or doubled hyphen,         then reservation, then the skeleton claim, then the cooldown. The         same structure check runs in `reserve`, `unreserve` and         `assignReserved`, so governance cannot reserve or assign a spelling         that `register` would refuse.         Skeleton: lowercased, `-` removed, then `0→o`, `1→l`, `i→l`, `5→s`,         `rn→m`, `vv→w`, applied in one pass over the hyphen-free bytes. The         skeleton is what the one-name-per-spelling rule keys on, so `alice`,         `a1ice`, `al-ice` and `allce` all claim the same name, and `cor-n`         folds with `corn`, not with `c0rn`.         Governance can reserve names and assign a reserved name to a Safe. It         has no path to change a registered entry's owner, version or records         location, and no way to release on someone else's behalf: there is         deliberately no such function. The only writer of an entry after         `register` is the owning Safe (`setRecordsLocation`, `release`).         State transitions (see the functions): `register` writes an entry;         `release` deletes it and starts the cooldown; a released handle keeps         its version in `lastVersion`, so a re-registration after the cooldown         gets version + 1 and a version is never reused.",
    "version": 1
  }
